  "methodological_hypotheses": {
    "linear_models_circularity": "Hypothesis / property: For linear models (Logistic Regression, Linear SVM), the deletion test is partly circular because exact token weights w_j * x_j are closed-form components of the logit. The most informative evaluations are therefore LIME vs exact and Random Forest vs the linear models.",
    "naive_bayes_lime_divergence": "Hypothesis: Naive Bayes is strictly additive in log-space (log-odds = b + sum x_j w_j). The lower rank correlation observed between exact NB log-odds and LIME is hypothesized to stem from two factors: (1) severe probability saturation in Naive Bayes where posterior probabilities cluster within 1e-6 of 0 or 1, producing a step-like probability surface under random token masking; and (2) objective discrepancy: LIME fits an L2-regularized linear surrogate predicting probability shifts, whereas exact attribution decomposes log-odds.",
    "tfidf_l2_rescaling_after_deletion": "Hypothesis: Deleting tokens from raw text induces TF-IDF L2-normalization rescaling: when a token is removed, the document vector norm decreases, mathematically boosting the normalized weights of all remaining terms by 1 / sqrt(1 - x_deleted^2). In emails with dozens of strong spam features, this rescaling can partially offset the drop from removing a single token at k=1.",
    "cross_model_tree_discrepancy": "Hypothesis: Random Forest relies on axis-aligned feature splits and decision paths that can capture nonlinear feature interactions. When explained via LIME, its feature attributions moderately correlate with linear models (rho ~ 0.46-0.50) but diverge from Complement NB (rho ~ -0.04)."
  },
